WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.708
Module 6: Mosaic Signal.

00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:06.120
A Culture Mosaic tells you where
a culture is strong or stuck.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.397
Mosaic Signal helps you see why.

00:00:08.397 --> 00:00:13.734
You hear it in people's own words, and
they tell you what those words mean.

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:19.142
In about seventy minutes you'll learn
how it works, how to pick a framework,

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:24.195
how to write a story prompt and
questions that produce useful patterns,

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:26.828
and how to read the results honestly.

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:33.281
Imagine your Culture Mosaic comes back and
Change Ready is one of the lowest tiles.

00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:36.792
You know there's a problem
with how change lands.

00:00:36.792 --> 00:00:42.310
What you don't know is what that problem
feels like on a Tuesday morning in a

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:46.610
clinic: whether people resist
change, can't keep up with it,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.831
or never hear about it in time.

00:00:48.831 --> 00:00:54.850
You could write more survey questions, but
you'd only be guessing at the right ones.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.573
Mosaic Signal takes a different route.

00:00:57.573 --> 00:01:02.231
It asks people to tell one short,
real story from their own work.

00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:07.964
Then it asks a few quick questions about
that story: what drove it, how it felt,

00:01:07.964 --> 00:01:10.257
and what was present or missing.

00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:15.130
Because people place their own
stories, the patterns come from them,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.999
not from a fixed list of
questions written in advance.

00:01:18.999 --> 00:01:23.944
And every dot on every chart links
back to a real story you can read.

00:01:23.944 --> 00:01:27.312
Breadth you can see across
hundreds of stories,

00:01:27.312 --> 00:01:30.393
and depth you can read
one story at a time.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.656
Here's the core principle.

00:01:32.656 --> 00:01:35.358
Respondents never rate the organization.

00:01:35.358 --> 00:01:37.451
They interpret their own story.

00:01:37.451 --> 00:01:42.382
Most surveys ask how much you agree
that leaders communicate change well.

00:01:42.382 --> 00:01:45.691
Mosaic Signal asks you to
tell a real experience,

00:01:45.691 --> 00:01:50.352
then to tell us about your story:
where it sits between three forces,

00:01:50.352 --> 00:01:55.080
where on a slider, which aspects were
present and whether they helped.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.714
That second step is what makes it work.

00:01:57.714 --> 00:02:01.293
The person who lived the
story decides what it means.

00:02:01.293 --> 00:02:06.899
Their answers can be plotted, so patterns
appear when many stories land in the same

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:07.304
place.

00:02:07.304 --> 00:02:10.006
And every point stays tied to its story.

00:02:10.006 --> 00:02:12.167
When you explain it to a client,

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:17.638
lead with the experience: people share
one short story and answer a few questions

00:02:17.638 --> 00:02:19.462
about it, in a few minutes.

00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:22.064
Here's what a respondent sees.

00:02:22.064 --> 00:02:27.238
First, a story prompt: one clear
invitation to describe a real experience.

00:02:27.238 --> 00:02:32.271
They type their story, and you can
set a minimum and maximum word count.

00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:35.208
Next come the questions about their story.

00:02:35.208 --> 00:02:37.934
A triangle with three labelled corners,

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:42.409
where they place a dot to show
the balance between three forces.

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:44.227
A slider between two ends.

00:02:44.227 --> 00:02:49.820
A small grid, where they drag a few named
items to show how present each was and

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:50.659
how it felt.

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:56.112
Last, a couple of simple context questions,
like how long they've worked here,

00:02:56.112 --> 00:02:58.349
so you can compare groups later.

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:00.796
That's it: a few minutes per story.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.358
There are four question types.

00:03:03.358 --> 00:03:05.484
The narrative is the story box,

00:03:05.484 --> 00:03:10.559
and its design rule is to ask for one
concrete experience, not an opinion.

00:03:10.559 --> 00:03:14.125
The triad is a triangle with
three labelled corners.

00:03:14.125 --> 00:03:19.269
The closer the dot is to a corner, the
more that force dominated the story.

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:24.550
Pick three things that compete for
something limited, like time or attention.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.802
A good triad reveals tensions
people can feel but rarely name.

00:03:28.802 --> 00:03:30.997
It also works fully by keyboard.

00:03:30.997 --> 00:03:34.220
The dyad is a slider
between two opposite ends.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.581
Make both ends places a
real person would choose.

00:03:37.581 --> 00:03:43.273
Most people land somewhere in the middle,
and where the middle sits is the finding.

00:03:43.273 --> 00:03:48.280
And stones are a small two-axis grid,
where respondents drag named items,

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.395
for example how present each
one was and how well it worked.

00:03:52.395 --> 00:03:54.590
Use three to five items at most.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:00.256
Every Mosaic Signal survey starts from
one of four ready-made frameworks,

00:04:00.256 --> 00:04:05.482
or a blank canvas, and you can edit
every question once the survey exists.

00:04:05.482 --> 00:04:09.650
The Organizational Culture
Probe is for stories about work,

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.887
moving between individual, team
and organizational dynamics.

00:04:13.887 --> 00:04:18.761
The Customer Experience Journey maps
customers' stories across speed,

00:04:18.761 --> 00:04:20.173
quality and empathy.

00:04:20.173 --> 00:04:24.693
The Change Readiness Assessment
asks whether people are willing,

00:04:24.693 --> 00:04:26.671
able and ready for a change.

00:04:26.671 --> 00:04:30.979
And Quick Narrative Capture is
for fast collection at events,

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:32.745
workshops or site visits.

00:04:32.745 --> 00:04:35.853
Start from the closest
template and edit it.

00:04:35.853 --> 00:04:37.407
They're tested shapes.

00:04:37.407 --> 00:04:42.068
A blank survey is for experienced
designers with a clear question.

00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:43.555
Let's practise.

00:04:43.555 --> 00:04:48.235
For each of four client requests,
choose the template you'd start from.

00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:50.881
The prompt is the whole survey.

00:04:50.881 --> 00:04:55.383
Everything else describes the
story, so if the stories are vague,

00:04:55.383 --> 00:04:57.115
the patterns will be too.

00:04:57.115 --> 00:05:00.439
Ask for one concrete
experience, not an opinion.

00:05:00.439 --> 00:05:05.080
"What do you think about change at
Northwind?" asks for an opinion,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:08.128
and you'll get general
complaints or praise.

00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:13.739
Stronger: "Tell us about a time in the
last few months when a change at Northwind

00:05:13.739 --> 00:05:15.055
affected your work.

00:05:15.055 --> 00:05:19.696
What happened?" "Why is cross-site
collaboration poor?" is leading,

00:05:19.696 --> 00:05:21.774
because it assumes the answer.

00:05:21.774 --> 00:05:27.315
Stronger: "Tell us about a recent time
you needed something from another site or

00:05:27.315 --> 00:05:27.661
team.

00:05:27.661 --> 00:05:31.679
How did it go?" A good prompt
points to a specific moment,

00:05:31.679 --> 00:05:37.082
sets a time window that keeps memories
fresh, is neutral enough to allow good,

00:05:37.082 --> 00:05:41.584
bad and mixed stories, asks what
happened rather than who did it,

00:05:41.584 --> 00:05:44.285
and is easy to answer in a few minutes.

00:05:44.285 --> 00:05:48.580
Keep minimum word counts low
enough that people don't give up.

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:54.163
For triads, pick three things that
compete for the same limited thing,

00:05:54.163 --> 00:05:56.930
and make all three corners reasonable.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.808
If one corner is obviously the
right answer, everyone will pick it.

00:06:01.808 --> 00:06:03.992
Willing, Able and Ready works,

00:06:03.992 --> 00:06:09.089
because a story can lean toward "I
wanted to" without being "I could".

00:06:09.089 --> 00:06:13.821
For dyads, both ends should be
places a real person would choose,

00:06:13.821 --> 00:06:15.205
with no better end.

00:06:15.205 --> 00:06:17.971
Waited to see, versus jumped in early.

00:06:17.971 --> 00:06:24.014
For stones, three to five items at most,
with both ends of each axis clearly named.

00:06:24.014 --> 00:06:25.034
One watch-out.

00:06:25.034 --> 00:06:29.839
Standalone Mosaic Signal surveys
are written for open exploration,

00:06:29.839 --> 00:06:34.062
so dyads have no better end
and triads have no bad corner.

00:06:34.062 --> 00:06:39.959
In Culture Dig, the same question types
are given fixed directions so they can be

00:06:39.959 --> 00:06:40.469
scored.

00:06:40.469 --> 00:06:42.435
Don't mix the two purposes.

00:06:42.933 --> 00:06:44.400
Your turn to design.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:50.048
Alex Moreau says clinic staff feel like
a different company since the merger,

00:06:50.048 --> 00:06:55.696
and the Culture Mosaic showed Collaboration
and Change Ready among the lowest

00:06:55.696 --> 00:06:58.996
tiles, with the Clinic
Network lowest of all.

00:06:58.996 --> 00:07:04.571
Draft one story prompt, one triad and
one dyad for a survey of clinic staff.

00:07:04.571 --> 00:07:09.265
Then reveal the model answer and
score yourself with the rubric.

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:12.891
Here's the path from
purchase to live survey.

00:07:12.891 --> 00:07:18.377
Sign in first: Mosaic Signal is bought
signed in, by an editor, admin or owner.

00:07:18.377 --> 00:07:19.557
Pick a framework.

00:07:19.557 --> 00:07:23.932
Say how many people you'll ask,
which sets the respondent band,

00:07:23.932 --> 00:07:27.265
and the survey closes to
new answers at its cap.

00:07:27.265 --> 00:07:31.709
Enter your partner code only if
you're buying for your own work.

00:07:31.709 --> 00:07:36.847
Once paid, the template is copied into
your organization and appears under

00:07:36.847 --> 00:07:37.542
Purchases.

00:07:37.542 --> 00:07:42.888
Open it, edit the prompt and questions
in the builder, preview it on a phone,

00:07:42.888 --> 00:07:46.499
then publish and share it
by link, QR code or email.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:47.818
Remember the rules.

00:07:47.818 --> 00:07:51.568
Launch within about two
months, or the survey expires.

00:07:51.568 --> 00:07:53.443
One purchase is one survey.

00:07:53.443 --> 00:07:58.303
The respondent cap can be lowered
but not raised past what was bought.

00:07:58.303 --> 00:08:00.178
It never joins a benchmark.

00:08:00.178 --> 00:08:04.553
And up to three extra waves can
be bought at checkout or later,

00:08:04.553 --> 00:08:09.205
each started from the launch's card
on Purchases with "Start wave".

00:08:09.205 --> 00:08:14.968
If a partner code paid for the launch, the
partner portal shows which wave it's on.

00:08:15.467 --> 00:08:17.638
Set expectations before launch.

00:08:17.638 --> 00:08:20.229
Mosaic Signal is not a scored survey.

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:25.131
There's no designed PowerPoint deck
and no zero-to-one-hundred scores.

00:08:25.131 --> 00:08:29.612
You work from the charts and
stories in SurveyCraft's analytics.

00:08:29.612 --> 00:08:31.853
There's no benchmark, by design.

00:08:31.853 --> 00:08:37.386
When we say "a cluster", we mean a visible
concentration of dots that you read,

00:08:37.386 --> 00:08:39.907
not something an algorithm computed.

00:08:39.907 --> 00:08:44.949
And add-ons, a Mosaic readout, custom
framework design, and extra waves,

00:08:44.949 --> 00:08:48.940
are separate, bought at checkout
or later from Purchases.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:54.753
If a client needs a scored, benchmarked
view of culture, that's the Culture Mosaic.

00:08:54.753 --> 00:08:58.464
Mosaic Signal is what you
add when they need the why.

00:08:58.967 --> 00:09:03.208
Each question type has its own
view in SurveyCraft's analytics.

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:07.583
A triad is a triangle with a dot
per story and an average marker,

00:09:07.583 --> 00:09:12.161
and you can switch to a density view
once there are three responses.

00:09:12.161 --> 00:09:16.065
Look at where dots concentrate,
and which corner is empty.

00:09:16.065 --> 00:09:19.431
A dyad shows the distribution
of slider positions.

00:09:19.431 --> 00:09:22.663
Look at its shape: one
hump, two humps, or flat.

00:09:22.663 --> 00:09:27.173
Stones show where each item sits,
and whether placements are split.

00:09:27.173 --> 00:09:31.481
And the narrative explorer lets
you search and read the stories.

00:09:31.481 --> 00:09:34.309
Click any dot to read the story behind it.

00:09:34.309 --> 00:09:36.261
Read a pattern in four steps.

00:09:36.261 --> 00:09:38.752
Look at the shape before the average.

00:09:38.752 --> 00:09:43.733
Name what's surprising, like "almost
nobody placed their story near Able".

00:09:43.733 --> 00:09:48.984
Read at least ten to fifteen stories in
that area before you name the pattern.

00:09:48.984 --> 00:09:52.283
Then write the pattern in
the respondents' terms.

00:09:52.767 --> 00:09:58.488
Northwind ran a Change Readiness Assessment
for nursing and clinic staff in March

00:09:58.488 --> 00:10:02.513
and April, and collected a
hundred and forty-two stories.

00:10:02.513 --> 00:10:07.811
On the triad, most dots sit along the
Willing edge, and very few near Able.

00:10:07.811 --> 00:10:12.048
The "waited to see, or jumped
in early" slider has one hump,

00:10:12.048 --> 00:10:14.308
leaning toward jumping in early.

00:10:14.308 --> 00:10:20.100
The "official announcements, or word of
mouth" slider has two humps: about four in

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:23.208
ten near each end, with
a mean of fifty-two.

00:10:23.208 --> 00:10:27.940
On the stones, Vision is present,
Skills are present and effective,

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.047
Resources are mostly
absent and ineffective,

00:10:31.047 --> 00:10:34.791
and Support is split: clinic
stories place it absent,

00:10:34.791 --> 00:10:37.192
hospital stories place it present.

00:10:37.192 --> 00:10:41.500
Two stories from the "willing,
not able" area say it plainly.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:45.667
Staff were told to use the new
scheduling system on Monday,

00:10:45.667 --> 00:10:48.281
and nobody had logins until Thursday.

00:10:48.281 --> 00:10:53.366
Now judge five statements about these
results: supported, not supported,

00:10:53.366 --> 00:10:54.990
or needs more evidence.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.462
Watch out for the mean
that hides two camps.

00:10:58.462 --> 00:11:03.780
A slider average of fifty can mean everyone
placed their story near the middle,

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:07.212
or half placed it at one
end and half at the other.

00:11:07.212 --> 00:11:10.309
The average is the same,
and the story is not.

00:11:10.309 --> 00:11:14.550
So always look at the distribution
before you quote an average.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:19.329
If there are two humps, report both
camps and read the stories in each.

00:11:19.329 --> 00:11:21.550
The split is usually the finding.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:23.973
The same goes for triads and stones.

00:11:24.467 --> 00:11:28.963
Time for the module check: eight
questions, and you need seven right.

00:11:28.963 --> 00:11:30.396
Retakes are unlimited.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:35.809
Here's your Mosaic Signal Design
Guide: which framework to start from,

00:11:35.809 --> 00:11:40.157
how to write the prompt and
questions, a pre-launch checklist,

00:11:40.157 --> 00:11:41.840
and how to read results.

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:42.681
Download it.

00:11:43.167 --> 00:11:43.851
To recap.

00:11:43.851 --> 00:11:49.782
Mosaic Signal collects real stories and
lets people interpret their own story,

00:11:49.782 --> 00:11:52.139
so the patterns come from them.

00:11:52.139 --> 00:11:57.081
There are four question types,
narrative, triad, dyad and stones,

00:11:57.081 --> 00:11:59.362
and four templates plus blank.

00:11:59.362 --> 00:12:05.369
Good design means one concrete experience
in the prompt, triads with competing,

00:12:05.369 --> 00:12:11.071
reasonable corners, sliders with two
honest ends, and three to five stones.

00:12:11.071 --> 00:12:15.709
One purchase is one survey,
launched within about two months,

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:18.675
with no scored report and no benchmark.

00:12:18.675 --> 00:12:24.453
And read shape before average, and read
the stories before naming a pattern.

00:12:24.453 --> 00:12:26.582
Next: Module 7, Culture Dig.

